      },
      {
        "id": "constructor-start",
        "says": "Starting a thread from a constructor can expose a half-initialised object, so a factory method starts it instead.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a publication hazard: no test can make a thread observe a half-built object every time, so it cannot be graded without luck"
      },
      {
        "id": "daemon-abandoned",
        "says": "At JVM exit daemon threads are abandoned abruptly: they are not interrupted and their finally blocks do not run.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Interview Q&A Section"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "daemon-before-start",
        "says": "A daemon thread does not keep the JVM alive, and daemon status can only be set before start().",
        "basis": [
          "example:20-thread-basics/README_6.1.1.md:22",
          "tests:20-thread-basics/src/test/java/com/github/msorkhpar/claudejavatutor/threadbasics/ThreadCreationTest.java"
        ],
        "exercise": "daemons",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "lambda-task",
        "says": "Runnable is a functional interface, so a lambda can be the task a thread runs.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Method 3: Lambda Expression (Java 8+)"
        ],
        "exercise": "launch",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "naming",
        "says": "Threads are named at construction or by a builder with a counting suffix, which makes thread dumps and logs readable.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques",
          "example:20-thread-basics/README_6.1.1.md:26"
        ],
        "exercise": "factory",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "prefer-pools",
        "says": "Production code prefers thread pools to creating raw threads, which is expensive, especially in loops.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "advice only here; executors and thread pools are the subject of module 23 (Executors)"
      },
      {
        "id": "priority",
        "says": "Priority runs from MIN_PRIORITY 1 to MAX_PRIORITY 10, defaults to 5, and is only a hint to the scheduler.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember",
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling",
          "tests:20-thread-basics/src/test/java/com/github/msorkhpar/claudejavatutor/threadbasics/ThreadCreationTest.java"
        ],
        "exercise": "factory",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "restore-interrupt",
        "says": "A caught InterruptedException is not swallowed: the interrupt status is restored.",
        "basis": [
          "example:20-thread-basics/README_6.1.1.md:10"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "interruption and cooperative cancellation are the subject of 6.1.3 (Stopping threads), whose practices check it"
      },
      {
        "id": "run-vs-start",
        "says": "Calling run() executes the task on the calling thread; start() creates a new thread that runs it and returns at once.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember",
          "example:20-thread-basics/README_6.1.1.md:16"
        ],
        "exercise": "launch",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "runnable-preferred",
        "says": "Implementing Runnable is preferred to extending Thread, because it leaves the inheritance slot free and separates the task from how it runs.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Method 2: Implementing Runnable",
          "example:20-thread-basics/README_6.1.1.md:14"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "start-twice",
        "says": "Starting the same Thread instance twice throws IllegalThreadStateException.",
        "basis": [
          "example:20-thread-basics/README_6.1.1.md:18"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "practised on 6.1.2 by 'Keep a job running without starting a thread twice', the page whose subject is start()"
      },
      {
        "id": "virtual-threads",
        "says": "Virtual threads are cheap JVM-managed threads suited to I/O-bound work, while CPU-bound work stays on platform threads.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Method 4: Virtual Threads (Java 21)",
          "example:20-thread-basics/README_6.1.1.md:20"
